From 246baa51b8ce19b0fd6641f43920fff3148c398e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:49:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wanduck: reset the primary's counter on dual-WAN fail-back

In fail-back mode the primary line's changed_count[] doubles as the
fail-back counter while the backup is active, and counts up to
wandog_fb_count (default 4). The return-to-primary block called
switch_wan_line(primary, 0) without resetting it, so the primary became
the current line already holding 4, which is at or above
wandog_maxfail (default 2).

conn_state_old[] of the primary still reads DISCONN from the original
outage, so a single disconnected scan right after fail-back (the
default route has not moved yet, because restart_wan_line is not
waited on) took the DISCONN branch: switch_wan_line(backup, 1), whose
restart_other loop issues restart_wan_if <primary> and kills the fresh
PPPoE session. The primary then recovers, fails back, and the cycle
repeats. Unplugging the backup stops it only because a disconnected
primary is not counted while the other line has no link.

Field report: GT-BE98, PPPoE on WAN plus a LAN-port WAN in fail-back
mode. Stock ASUS logic, present on every model.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 release/src/router/rc/wanduck.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/release/src/router/rc/wanduck.c b/release/src/router/rc/wanduck.c
index 3693763d4e..ae6103de75 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rc/wanduck.c
+++ b/release/src/router/rc/wanduck.c
@@ -5193,6 +5193,14 @@ if(test_log) _dprintf("nat_rule: stop_nat_rules 7.\n");
 
 				handle_wan_line(other_wan_unit, rule_setup);
 				switch_wan_line(other_wan_unit, 0);
+				/* reaper (field 2026-09-25, GT-BE98): changed_count[] of the primary doubles as
+				 * the fail-back counter above and was never reset here, so the primary became the
+				 * current line already holding wandog_fb_count (4) >= wandog_maxfail (2). One
+				 * DISCONN scan right after fail-back (default route not yet moved) then tripped
+				 * the DISCONN branch -> switch_wan_line(backup, 1) -> restart_wan_if <primary>,
+				 * killing the fresh PPPoE session in an endless fail-back loop. Start clean.
+				 */
+				set_disconn_count(other_wan_unit, S_IDLE);
 			}
 			else if(conn_state[other_wan_unit] == PHY_RECONN){
 				_dprintf("\n# wanduck(fail-back): Try to prepare the backup line.\n");
